<!--
  PHP2Shell (CVE-2026-109618 / CVE-2026-109619) memory-exhaustion signal on
  Apache mod_php, for Wazuh. Tested on Wazuh 4.14.7 against PHP 8.5.11.

  SIGNAL
  Apache writes the PHP bailout to error.log wrapped in its own prefix:

    [php:error] PHP Fatal error: Allowed memory size ... exhausted

  That line is decoded by stock rule 30301 at level 0, so nothing alerts on it
  by default. This rule is a child of 30301 that raises the level.

  WHAT STOCK WAZUH 4.14.7 DID NOT CATCH, AND WHY
  31122 is web 500, and the observed response was HTTP 200, so a rule keyed on
  the status code misses this entirely. 31420 expects a bare "PHP Fatal error"
  line and does not fire on the Apache-wrapped form. 30301 and 31108 both
  decoded the events, at level 0. Collection was never the problem; the level
  was.

  TUNING
  Change the id if 102005 is taken in your ruleset. The match is deliberately
  the broad "PHP Fatal error", which is what was validated. If your
  applications log fatals routinely, narrow it to "Allowed memory size" to key
  on the exhaustion specifically, at the cost of missing other fatal classes
  this vulnerability can produce.
-->
<group name="web,apache,php,">

  <rule id="102005" level="10">
    <if_sid>30301</if_sid>
    <match>PHP Fatal error</match>
    <description>Apache PHP Fatal error, possible PHP2Shell memory exhaustion</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1499.004</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
